<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rijha Safdar
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Danyail Mateen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Syed Taha Ali
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wajahat Hussain
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>R. Safdar, S.T. Ali and W. Hussain are with School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, National University of Sciences and Technology, Islamabad, Pakistan, 44000. e-mail: rsafdar.dphd19seecs@seecs.edu.pk ,e-mail: taha.ali@seecs.edu.pk, email:wajahat.hussain@seecs.edu.pk</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>D. Mateen is with the Department
Computer Science, Fast University, Islamabad,
Pakistan, 44000</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Artificial Intelligence (AI) and more specifically Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated exceptional progress in multiple areas including software engineering, however, their capability for vulnerability detection in the wild scenario and its corresponding reasoning remains underexplored. Prompting pre-trained LLMs in an effective way offers a computationally effective and scalable solution. Our contributions are (i)varied prompt designs for vulnerability detection and its corresponding reasoning in the wild. (ii)a real-world vector data store constructed from the National Vulnerability Database, that will provide real time context to vulnerability detection framework, and (iii)a scoring measure for combined measurement of accuracy and reasoning quality. Our contribution aims to examine whether LLMs are ready for wild deployment, thus enabling the reliable use of LLMs stronger for the development of secure software’s.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>LLMs, In-context Evaluation, Prompt Engineering, Real-World,Wild, Vulnerability Detection, Reasoning

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs based on the transformers architecture and pre-trained on large scale, diverse datasets have shown exceptional capabilities across variety of natural language processing (NLP) tasks, especially their decoder-only models such as GPT-4<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, DeepSeekR1<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Claude<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and Qwen2.5-Coder<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which have shown remarkable performance in generative reasoning and understanding. Software source code vulnerability detection can be framed as an NLP task due to structural similarities, and recent research has applied transformers based model for the task of source code analysis, including vulnerability detection<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Software vulnerabilities, when exploited, cause serious damage. As an example in 2021, the Log4Shell vulnerability (CVE-2021-44228) in Apache Log4j <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> exposed billions of devices to remote code execution attacks, affecting major tech companies like Apple, Amazon, and Microsoft, with an estimated global impact exceeding $10 billion. Similarly, in 2023, one of the largest security breaches was caused by a critical SQL injection vulnerability (CVE-2023-34362) in MOVEit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, a popular file transfer tool. It was exploited by the CLOP ransomware group. This single vulnerability breached the personal data of 77 million people across 2,600 organizations, causing over $12 billion in global damage and seriously impacting U.S. government agencies and entire state systems. By using a proactive approach to detect vulnerabilities before they are exploited, organizations can help protect reputation, reduce costs, and safeguard sensitive data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional methods<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> often miss complex or subtle flaws/vulnerabilities. In contrast, LLMs with their attention mechanism<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, diverse pretraining and reasoning capability offer a promising direction for vulnerability detection and mitigation. However, despite their capabilities, a key question is how robust are they in practice?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate, consider the example in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where a vulnerable function is misclassified as secure after only minor edits such as adding a benign library include or renaming variables. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> research findings reflect significant non-robustness in even the most capable models like ‘PaLM2’ and ‘GPT-4’. These models yield incorrect results 26% and 17%. These finding suggest that further advancement in LLMs is necessary before they can be used as general purpose security assistants.
So, are current models truly ready for practical, in-context vulnerability detection and reasoning in the wild?</p>
</div>
