</div>
<div id="lstnumberx15" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;">}</span>
</div>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustrative example of LLM robustness limitations .
In (a), the vulnerability is correctly flagged; in (b), after adding a harmless library include or variable renaming, the vulnerability persists but models often misclassify it as secure.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Existing research focuses on either fine-tuning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> or prompting LLMs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> for vulnerability detection. Some recent benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> attempt to evaluate LLM for vulnerability detection. These benchmarks limit LLM capability by relying on predefined, known vulnerabilities, which prevents evaluation on unseen or real-world code and real-world context scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and thus fail to reflect the complexity of real-world vulnerability detection. These approaches often suffer from key short-comings such as the use of limited datasets rather than real-world data, unrealistic scenario where vulnerability is pre-defined, and a lack of real-world experimental evaluation using real-world context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With the rapid progress of LLM capabilities, hundreds of models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> are released over the past four years, signifying the need for a standardized, real-world benchmarking framework to assess their effectiveness in vulnerability detection and its corresponding reasoning. We propose a systematic real-world in-context based evaluation framework to assess capability of LLMs for vulnerability detection and reason of vulnerability. The practical framework incorporates diverse prompting strategies, real-world retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to provide real-world context enabling LLMs to reason more effectively over complex code scenarios.
In vulnerability detection, an accurate prediction without correct reasoning can be misleading. Existing metrics evaluation is based on either prediction or reasoning independently, overlooking partial correctness and a combined scoring metrics. This motivated us to design a hybrid metric that captures both. So, we introduce a novel scoring metric that jointly evaluates the correctness of vulnerability detection and the quality of reasoning, offering a more realistic and comprehensive assessment. Partial credit is awarded for LLM for giving correct prediction but incomplete or incorrect reasoning. By doing so, we move beyond binary labels accuracy or reasoning correctness separately, toward a richer more informative score of model behavior.
Our approach is focused on wild scenarios where no prior knowledge of the vulnerability within a given code snippet is known beforehand unlike prior work<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. This closely reflects practical scenario faced by a security analysts. We leverage comprehensive, authentic, real-world publicly available vulnerability data from National Vulnerability Database (NVD)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> for dynamic in-context learning via retrieval. Our evaluation framework assesses five state-of-the-art LLMs from OpenAI, Meta, Alibaba, Microsoft, and Google utilizing four structured prompting paradigms which are standard, chain-of-thought, decomposition, and plan-and-solve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions of the paper are:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Development of a <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">novel real-world</span> evaluation framework, Real-VulLLM, a novel in-context based evaluation framework for LLM. The framework systematically detects and reasons about real-world vulnerability with real-world context under varied prompting styles across five state-of-the-art models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build a real-world vectorized knowledge base data store from the NVD, transforming its CVE entries, patch codes, and commit metadata into high-dimensional embeddings. The vectorized store enables semantic similarity search and real-time retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). This enables dynamic in-context learning with real-world dynamic, comprehensive, and high-impact vulnerabilities, thereby providing deeper insights about behavior of LLM under realistic, security-critical conditions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a hybrid evaluation scoring metric that combines the accuracy of the prediction and the quality of the reasoning. It also introduces a partial correctness score to credit partially correct information. We also identify the best prompts that can be utilized for optimal performance in practical vulnerability detection and its reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">The proposed unconstrained evaluation framework targets vulnerability detection and reasoning in the wild, where neither the presence nor the nature of a vulnerability is known a priori.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our initial evaluation shows that vulnerability detection without knowledge augmentation coincides with the prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, confirming that even advanced LLMs struggle under the constrained conditions. Incorporating knowledge augmentation through CVE based retrieval showed improved performance than zero-shot scenario, particularly for models with strong reasoning capabilities. For example, GPT-4 showed a notable increase in performance when paired with structured prompts like decomposition and plan-and-solve, due to its strong reasoning abilities. In contrast, Qwen2.5-Coder’s performance, on the other hand, declined despite code specific training highlights difficulty to filter relevant contextual information and limited vulnerability reasoning ability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Fig.<a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III Real-VulLLM ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the Real-VulLLM pipeline. It incorporates multiple wild prompts for both zero-shot and in-context evaluation. CVE data from NVD is transformed into a vectorized knowledge store (RAG), relevant samples are retrieved on the basis of semantic similarity. Five LLMs are evaluated across four prompting strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation includes both zero-shot and in-context, covering over 15 real-world critical vulnerabilities and their corresponding patches, across five prominent LLMs. This allowed us to quantify the effect of structured prompt paired with real-world context, which only help when the model has capacity to interpret it effectively. An example of evaluation process is shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of the paper is organized as follows: Section II provides background and related work. Section III presents real-world evaluation framework. Section IV discusses results of experiments and insights. Section V concludes the paper and provides future research directions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="103.19" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 103.19" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,103.19) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000CC;" fill="#0000CC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.84 L 0 98.35 C 0 101.02 2.17 103.19 4.84 103.19 L 645.16 103.19 C 647.83 103.19 650 101.02 650 98.35 L 650 4.84 C 650 2.17 647.83 0 645.16 0 L 4.84 0 C 2.17 0 0 2.17 0 4.84 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B4D2FF;" fill="#B4D2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.84 L 0.69 81.75 L 649.31 81.75 L 649.31 4.84 C 649.31 2.55 647.45 0.69 645.16 0.69 L 4.84 0.69 C 2.55 0.69 0.69 2.55 0.69 4.84 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.55 89.07)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.18" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="545.19"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
