<span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question:<span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Does this code contain instances of security vulnerability or not?
Format your response strictly as:
<span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Prediction: [Yes/No]</span>
<span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Reason: [Explain in under 50 words]</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Code:<span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S1.F2.pic3.2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_C ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;"><span class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,dm9pZCBjb3B5X2lucHV0KGNoYXIgKnNyYykgewogICAgY2hhciBidWZbMTBdOwogICAgc3RyY3B5KGJ1Ziwgc3JjKTsgLy8gUG90ZW50aWFsIG92ZXJmbG93Cn0=" download="">⬇</a></span>
<span id="lstnumberx16" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">void</span><span id="lstnumberx16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">copy_input</span><span id="lstnumberx16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><span id="lstnumberx16.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">char</span><span id="lstnumberx16.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx16.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">*</span><span id="lstnumberx16.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">src</span><span id="lstnumberx16.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span><span id="lstnumberx16.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx16.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx17" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">char</span><span id="lstnumberx17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx17.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">buf</span><span id="lstnumberx17.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[10];</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx18" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">strcpy</span><span id="lstnumberx18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><span id="lstnumberx18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">buf</span><span id="lstnumberx18.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,</span><span id="lstnumberx18.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx18.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">src</span><span id="lstnumberx18.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">);</span><span id="lstnumberx18.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx18.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">//<span id="lstnumberx18.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Potential<span id="lstnumberx18.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>overflow</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx19" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">}</span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F2.pic4" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="20.79" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 9.75 20.79" width="9.75"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#505050;--ltx-fill-color:#505050;" fill="#505050" stroke="#505050" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="translate(0,20.79) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(4.87,0) translate(0,20.24)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 -18.58"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#505050;" color="#505050" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -19.13)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -3.54 4.32 C -2.9 1.73 -1.45 0.5 0 0 C -1.45 -0.5 -2.9 -1.73 -3.54 -4.32"></path></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F2.pic5" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="150.31" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 150.31" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,150.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B35900;" fill="#B35900" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.84 L 0 145.47 C 0 148.14 2.17 150.31 4.84 150.31 L 645.16 150.31 C 647.83 150.31 650 148.14 650 145.47 L 650 4.84 C 650 2.17 647.83 0 645.16 0 L 4.84 0 C 2.17 0 0 2.17 0 4.84 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFAD2;" fill="#FFFAD2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.84 L 0.69 128.87 L 649.31 128.87 L 649.31 4.84 C 649.31 2.55 647.45 0.69 645.16 0.69 L 4.84 0.69 C 2.55 0.69 0.69 2.55 0.69 4.84 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.55 136.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.18" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="545.19"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.F2.pic5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.4em;">
<span id="S1.F2.pic5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F2.pic5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">LLM Response</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.55 10.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.31em;--ltx-fo-height:7.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="109.24" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 109.24)" width="626.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.F2.pic5.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.31em;">
<span id="S1.F2.pic5.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F2.pic5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prediction:<span id="S1.F2.pic5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Yes</span></span></span>
<span id="S1.F2.pic5.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F2.pic5.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Reason:<span id="S1.F2.pic5.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The code uses <span id="S1.F2.pic5.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">strcpy()</span> without checking the input length, which can cause a buffer overflow. This is a classic case of CWE-121: Stack-Based Buffer Overflow.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt Template: The LLM is provided with a structured instruction, a system prompt assigning role of security expert, a user-defined query, and a real-world code snippet. It outputs prediction and reason. Prediction identifies the vulnerability and reason present the cause/justification.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Background and Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditionally, software vulnerability detection has relied on static, dynamic, and hybrid analysis techniques. Static analysis tools perform analysis on source code without executing it, to identify insecure patterns. However, they can not interpret complex control and data flows, often resulting in high false positive rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. In contrast dynamic analysis, runs the code to capture security flaws more accurately <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> but the analysis is computationally expensive, time consuming and requires security expert to run analysis. Hybrid analysis combines both approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, yet it still demands extensive manual effort and domain expertise. The number of software along with complexity of code base continues to grow, the task of security assessment has emerged as a significant challenge for traditional methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Related work in Software Vulnerability Detection</span></figcaption><img src="2510.04056v1/RW2.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:234/275;" width="234" height="275" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The challenges has motivated the adoption of AI based techniques for vulnerability detection. Deep learning based (DL) techniques incorporating control flow and data flow based representations constructed by tools like Joern, have been effective techniques<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. While the approaches in literature show effective results on their trained data, failed to recognize same vulnerability in other projects showing lack of generalization and often producing high false positives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
After DL, comes LLMs uch as GPT-3, GPT-4 (OpenAI), Gemini (Google), Qwencoder (Alibaba) have recently demonstrated remarkable capabilities pushing boundaries in generating code. LLMs being trained on huge amount of data, finds applications in detecting, localizing and generating patch for software vulnerabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. LLMs are used in fine-tuning<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for vulnerability detection where the pre-trained model is fine-tuned on vulnerability specific dataset, and also prompt-based inference including zero-shot or few-shot learning using crafted prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
In addition, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged to aid LLM by providing contextual domain specific information such as open-domain QA and medical reasoning<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Though effective approach for detecting vulnerabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> but it is underutilized. Rich resource based platform NVD containing detailed vulnerability information can be utilized for real-world LLM based augmented pipelines.
Recent works such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> have considered evaluating LLMs for software vulnerability detection and reasoning across varying experimental setups. While efforts are promising as it includes diverse prompts, automated evaluation, evaluation metrics integration etc. but a key gaps is the real-world applicability and contextual reasoning. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> utilizes real-world data while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> only partially utilizes such data, often relying on limited datasets that do not reflect the actual complexity found in the wild.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> evaluated GPT-4 and CodeLLaMA on C/C++ and Java projects, emphasizing the limitations of LLM with context-dependent complex vulnerabilities. However the evaluation lack real-world scenario lacked a realistic setting in which vulnerabilities are unknown and also it lack real-world in-context evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> combine Capture-the-Flag and real-world sources to evaluate LLMs on binary as well as multi-class vulnerability classification but neither the framework is automated and nor utilizes real-world in-context evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> proposed a framework for the reasoning based evaluation of LLMs in smart contract security, influenced by domain knowledge. It lacked automated evaluation and multi-dimensional experimentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> evaluated wide range of LLMs on synthetic as well as real-world datasets, focusing on vulnerability detection and CWE classification, but did not include real-world in-context based evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> emphasized key limitation of LLMs that it often struggle to differentiate between vulnerable and patched code.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> although an improved evaluation framework but but it lacks alignment with real-world situation where where vulnerabilities are not explicitly known beforehand and similarly providing hand picked examples as real-world context does not mimic real-world situation. Also, their reliance on majority voting using ROUGE scores evaluations raises concern. ROUGE based n-gram matching fails to evaluates semantic reasoning and hence it is not a reliable measure for strong evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to these recent efforts, our approach addresses the prior limitation by incorporating real-world in-context based evaluation on real-world data for vulnerability detection, supporting diverse prompting strategies, and deploying automated, multidimensional evaluation (zero-shot and in-context). This framework also incorporates a metrics integration to evaluate both correctness.
The advancement in software vulnerability detection is summarized in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II Background and Related Work ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
In this paper we have utilized real-world vulnerability dataset containing both vulnerable and patched code snippets curated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. The dataset contains vulnerability reported in MITRE top CWE’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. This real-world data is used to evaluate model performance in practical and realistic settings. Also, GPT-4o-mini is used as an automated evaluator to identify the semantic correctness of vulnerability reasoning provided by other models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Real-VulLLM</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present Real-VulLLM, a fully automated, metrics integrated framework for evaluating LLMs on vulnerability detection and reasoning in real-world conditions Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III Real-VulLLM ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Unlike prior work that rely on binary accuracy only or hand-crafted examples, Real-VulLLM combines three features: (i) an end-to-end pipeline that transforms real-world CVEs and patches into a vectorized knowledge base for semantic retrieval, (ii) structured prompting strategies (standard, chain-of-thought, decomposition, and plan-and-solve) to guide step-wise reasoning, and (iii) an automated evaluator that jointly measures prediction accuracy, semantic reasoning quality, and partial correctness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The pipeline begins with real-world CVEs and their corresponding patched samples, which are collected and transformed into a vectorized knowledge base using embeddings and stored in a vector database. This enables semantic retrieval of vulnerability–patch pairs. To analyze a code sample, the retriever module fetches semantically similar examples from the vector store and it is provided as additional context. The context is incorporated into structured prompt templates under multiple prompting strategies, including standard, chain-of-thought, decomposition, and plan-and-solve. The enriched prompts are then passed to representative LLMs including Gemini, GPT, Claude, LLaMA, and Phi-4, Qwen for both zero-shot and few-shot settings to test their ability to detect vulnerabilities and provide reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the outputs are assessed by our evaluator module, which measures binary prediction accuracy, reasoning quality (via semantic alignment and embedding similarity), and partial correctness to capture incomplete but useful reasoning. This enables automated, consistent, and scalable evaluation of diverse LLMs under realistic and practical scenarios. In the following subsections, we describe each stage of the pipeline in detail, following the flow shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III Real-VulLLM ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Architecture of Real-VulLLM</span></figcaption><img src="2510.04056v1/Real-VulLLM.jpg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/85;" width="229" height="85" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dataset and Vector Store Construction</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) identifies and catalogs software vulnerabilities which are publicly accessible. We selected four real-world projects containing 15 CVEs from MITRE top 25 vulnerabilities (2024) are selected<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. The details of dataset are shown in Table  <a href="#S3.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ III-A Dataset and Vector Store Construction ‣ III Real-VulLLM ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.
Although NVD provides comprehensive CVE information, its structured format is designed primarily for human browsing and keyword based search, not for in-context reasoning by LLMs. The format is not directly ingested by LLMs for in-context reasoning. To bridge this gap, we transform CVE entries, patch codes, and commit metadata into high-dimensional vector embeddings, enabling semantic similarity search and RAG. This allows our framework to dynamically provide LLMs with contextually relevant, real-world vulnerability information during evaluation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>CVEs grouped by MITRE Ranking and CWE ID. CWE Types: <span id="S3.T1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CWE-787</span> = Out-of-Bounds Write, <span id="S3.T1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CWE-476</span> = NULL Pointer Dereference, <span id="S3.T1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CWE-190</span> = Integer Overflow, <span id="S3.T1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CWE-416</span> = Use After-Free.</figcaption>
