</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To operationalize the static NVD as an automated, knowledge base, we first transform its CVE entries, its corresponding patch, and metadata into vector embeddings. This vectorization enables semantic similarity search, which goes beyond the keyword based queries supported by the original NVD format. To build the vector data store, we used <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qdrant</span>, a free vector database optimized for handling high-dimensional embeddings, enabling efficient querying, indexing and context retrieval. For embedding generation, we employed OpenAI’s <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text-embedding-3-small</span> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, released in January 2024. It has performance comparable to large models, but is cheaper and moreefficient  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. The model generates 1,536 dimensional embeddings and supports a maximum context length of 8,191 tokens, and is cost-efficient at $0.02 per one million tokens. It is widely adopted in RAG pipelines and frameworks such as LangChain  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and LLaMAIndex  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, this model is well suited for large-scale semantic search. We generated embeddings for each chunk of code and description to enable fine-grained semantic retrieval.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To create semantically coherent segments of the code and descriptions in order to aid LLM, we used LangChain’s Semantic Chunker, backed by the same embedding model. These embeddings, stored in a Qdrant database with structured payloads, enable most semantically similar and relevant retrieval of examples. A structured prompt Fig.<a href="#S3.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ III-A Dataset and Vector Store Construction ‣ III Real-VulLLM ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> to aid in vulnerability detection and reasoning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">System Prompt Template</span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.F6.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_align_center ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx31" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx31.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;">sys</span><span id="lstnumberx31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;">=</span><span id="lstnumberx31.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_string ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#990099;">""</span><span id="lstnumberx31.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_string ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#990099;">"You<span id="lstnumberx31.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>are<span id="lstnumberx31.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>a<span id="lstnumberx31.6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>security<span id="lstnumberx31.6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>expert.<span id="lstnumberx31.6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Answer<span id="lstnumberx31.6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx31.6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>following<span id="lstnumberx31.6.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>question<span id="lstnumberx31.6.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>clearly<span id="lstnumberx31.6.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>and<span id="lstnumberx31.6.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>concisely.<span id="lstnumberx31.6.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>For<span id="lstnumberx31.6.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>your<span id="lstnumberx31.6.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>reference<span id="lstnumberx31.6.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>we<span id="lstnumberx31.6.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>have<span id="lstnumberx31.6.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>provided<span id="lstnumberx31.6.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>a<span id="lstnumberx31.6.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>similar<span id="lstnumberx31.6.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>example.<span id="lstnumberx31.6.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>The<span id="lstnumberx31.6.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>following<span id="lstnumberx31.6.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>example<span id="lstnumberx31.6.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>is<span id="lstnumberx31.6.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>structured<span id="lstnumberx31.6.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>as<span id="lstnumberx31.6.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Description,<span id="lstnumberx31.6.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Vulnerable<span id="lstnumberx31.6.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Code,<span id="lstnumberx31.6.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Fixed<span id="lstnumberx31.6.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Code<span id="lstnumberx31.6.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>and<span id="lstnumberx31.6.33" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>finally<span id="lstnumberx31.6.34" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Code<span id="lstnumberx31.6.35" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>to<span id="lstnumberx31.6.36" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>be<span id="lstnumberx31.6.37" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>evaluated.<span id="lstnumberx31.6.38" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>You<span id="lstnumberx31.6.39" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>just<span id="lstnumberx31.6.40" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>need<span id="lstnumberx31.6.41" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>to<span id="lstnumberx31.6.42" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>provide<span id="lstnumberx31.6.43" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>a<span id="lstnumberx31.6.44" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>concise<span id="lstnumberx31.6.45" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>answer<span id="lstnumberx31.6.46" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>containing<span id="lstnumberx31.6.47" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>prediction<span id="lstnumberx31.6.48" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>and<span id="lstnumberx31.6.49" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>reasoning<span id="lstnumberx31.6.50" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>of<span id="lstnumberx31.6.51" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>"</span><span id="lstnumberx31.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;">Code</span><span id="lstnumberx31.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;">to</span><span id="lstnumberx31.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;">be</span><span id="lstnumberx31.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#1A1A1A;">evaluated</span><span id="lstnumberx31.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_string ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#990099;">".</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx32" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx32.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#990099;">Context:\n{context}.</span><span id="lstnumberx32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#990099;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#990099;">"</span><span id="lstnumberx32.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_string ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#990099;">""</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompting Strategies</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the knowledge base is constructed, retrieved samples are incorporated into structured prompt templates. To replicate the behavior of security analysts, enhance LLM performance on vulnerability detection and reasoning tasks and find the best suited prompt. Each strategy incrementally encourages more structured, multi-step reasoning replicating security expert behavior in addition to standard prompting. Standard and Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting is also widely used in literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> but not fully evaluated for zero-shot and in-context based evaluated. Therefore, in addition to Standard and CoT, we also incorporates Decomposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> and Plan-and-Solve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> prompting strategy as they are known for their effectiveness in complex tasks. Details of prompting strategies are elaborated below:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard Prompt (P-S)</span>: A baseline prompt that asks the model to classify code as vulnerable or secure for ZS (P-S-ZS) and FS (P-S-FS) scenario.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-Thought Prompt (P-CoT)</span>: Enhances the standard prompt with a reasoning phrase (e.g., “Let’s think step by step”) to promote intermediate logical steps for ZS (P-CoT-ZS) and FS (P-CoT-FS) scenario.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposition Prompt (P-Decomp)</span>: Breaks the task into sub tasks and structured steps to understanding, locating, and reasoning about the vulnerable and secure code and reason about type of vulnerability before reaching a conclusion. It is executed for ZS (P-Decomp-ZS) and FS (P-Decomp-FS) scenario.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Plan-and-Solve Prompt (P-P&amp;S)</span>: Instructs the model to plan first and then execute it step-by-step to solve the problem for ZS (P-&amp;S-ZS) and FS (P-&amp;S-FS) scenario.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Large Language Models (LLMs)</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The enriched prompts are then evaluated using representative state-of-the-art LLMs, both open-source and proprietary. We evaluated five leading LLMs, including GPT-4 by OpenAI, Gemini-1.5-Flash-002 by Google, Qwen2.5-Coder-14B by Alibaba, Meta-LLaMA-3-8B by Meta, and Phi-4 by Microsoft. The selection includes both open-source and proprietary models. These models vary in context length (up to 1M tokens), accessibility (API vs. locally hosted), and strengths ranging from high reasoning ability to efficiency on limited hardware, as summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III-C Large Language Models (LLMs) ‣ III Real-VulLLM ‣ Real-VulLLM: An LLM Based Assessment Framework in the Wild" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE II</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of Evaluated LLMs (CW = context-window length)</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
