</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">LLaMA-3-8B</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Jul’24</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">8K</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Local</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.5.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:92.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.5.5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Strong performance &amp; efficiency</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Phi-4</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Dec’24</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">128K</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Local</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 2.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.6.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:92.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.6.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.5.6.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Lightweight, instruction-tuned</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These LLMs are known for their exceptional capabilities in NLP tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. Previous work, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, evaluated GPT-4 for LLM evaluation but lack real-world scenario. We include GPT-4 in our study due to its consistently higher performance model across literature. Gemini-1.5-Flash was selected for evaluation as it is the latest open-source model by Google and is known for its high performance. Phi-4, Qwen2.5-Coder and LLaMA-3 represents recent open-source models that balance capability with efficiency. They are chosen because they are capable models, particularly suited for local deployment due to smaller size and optimization for advanced reasoning with high quality quality data. Additionally, GPU constraints of 12 GB influenced our selection of smaller model variants for local execution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluator</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The final stage of Real-VulLLM is evaluation. To comprehensively evaluate LLM performance for the task of software vulnerability, we designed an evaluation pipeline combining prediction accuracy, reasoning quality, and partial credit for incomplete but partially correct answers. This approach draws inspiration from recent work such as MT-Bench, KoalaEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> that judges answering quality of LLMs through semantic comparison based on embeddings. However, existing methodologies mostly focus on binary correctness without addressing security tasks such as identifying the right CWE type or localization of flaw line.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To account for overall model performance, we use accuracy that measures whether the LLM correctly predicts if the code is vulnerable or secure, cosine similarity measures how semantically close the LLM’s reasoning explanation is to the ground truth (GT) and a <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Partial Correctness Score</span> (<span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pcs</span>) rewards when the LLM identifies some part of the vulnerability (e.g., correct CWE or location, but incomplete reasoning).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given an LLM response, we use <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gpt-4o-mini</span> with a structured prompt to extract: (i) a binary prediction indicating whether the code is vulnerable (“yes”), secure (“no”), or unable to answer (“n/a”); and (ii) a summary of the model’s reasoning. The extracted prediction is compared to the GT label to compute the binary accuracy score (<span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">acc</span>). To assess reasoning quality, we adopt two complementary approaches as semantic similarity evaluates correctness from a human-like perspective, while embedding similarity offers a scalable, closeness measure. Together, they provide a holistic assessment of reasoning quality.</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic alignment</span>: <span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-mini</span> is used to judge whether the LLM’s reasoning aligns with the GT reasoning (“yes”/“no”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Embedding similarity</span>: We compute cosine similarity (<span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cs</span>) between the reasoning embeddings of the LLM and the GT, using the <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text-embedding-3-small</span> model. This provides graded semantic similarity.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The final evaluation metric combines all components into the <span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scoring Metrics (SM)</span> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SM}=\min\Big(1.0,\;(w_{1}\times\text{acc})+(w_{2}\times\text{cs})+(w_{3}\times\text{pcs})\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SM</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>acc</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>cs</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pcs</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SM}=\min\Big(1.0,\;(w_{1}\times\text{acc})+(w_{2}\times\text{cs})+(w_{3}\times\text{pcs})\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}=0.6</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{3}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{3}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. The final score is capped at 1.0 to maintain interpretability. This scoring metric enables holistic evaluation of LLMs across prediction correctness and reasoning quality in vulnerability detection tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To further illustrate the significance of our metric, consider a case involving a <span id="S3.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CWE-787</span> (Out-of-Bounds Write) vulnerability. The GT is that the code is “vulnerable” and the cause of error is writing beyond allocated memory. Suppose we have three models i.e., Model A, Model B, Model C. Model A predicts “secure” with high confidence, giving acc = 0 and SM <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Model B correctly predicts “vulnerable” but provides only a generic explanation (“code may cause a crash”), yielding acc = 1 but low semantic alignment (cosine similarity = 0.2, PCS = 0.3), resulting in SM = 0.69. Model C also predicts “vulnerable,” but with reasoning closely matching the GT (cosine similarity = 0.85, PCS = 0.85), leading to SM <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.94</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.94</annotation></semantics></math>. While binary accuracy alone treats Models B and C as equally correct, our scoring metric distinguishes them by incorporating semantic similarity. In additional, it credits cases where the reasoning is partially correct, instead of relying solely on the final label and also by rewarding semantically faithful reasoning and partial correctness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiment: Vulnerability Detection and Reasoning in the Wild</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate LLMs under realistic conditions with real-world data, we designed an experiment that aims to explore the capability of LLMs to detect and reason about software vulnerabilities. This experiment highlights how well model predict and explain about real-world vulnerabilities when evaluated with diverse and previously unseen code examples, thus moving beyond handcrafted or synthetic benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Correct Predictions with Correct Reasoning — Heatmap (Models × CVEs)</span></figcaption><img src="2510.04056v1/heatmap.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/129;" width="429" height="129" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experiment is conducted in two settings: Zero-Shot (ZS) and Few-Shot with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (FS).</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ZS</span> setting, model is asked to identify whether the code is vulnerable or not? If vulnerable, then identify location and reason about vulnerability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FS</span> setting, weuse Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), where semantically similar examples from a curated vector data store comprising vulnerable-patch pairs. The examples are fetched dynamically while assessment on the basis of embedding similarity and incorporated into the prompt as context. This setting enables automated real-time knowledge augmentation, maintaining a real-world setting.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The dataset includes 15 top-ranked CVEs from MITRE, each consisting of both a vulnerable and a patched version, for a total of 30 code snippets. For each snippet, we queried five LLMs (GPT-4, Gemini-1.5-Flash-002, Qwen2.5-Coder-14B, Meta-LLaMA-3-8B, and Phi-4) under both ZS and FS settings, with two independent queries per case to account for stochastic variation. This resulted in 240 runs per model (15 CVEs × 2 samples per CVE × 2 settings × 2 queries). This dual querying per sample helps in capturing variation in model behavior and get an insight that applying context will help the model in identifying vulnerability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results and Analysis</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 8</span>: </span><span id="S4.F8.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S4.F8.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Performance on Vulnerability Detection and Reasoning.</span>
Each model has two grouped horizontal bars representing Zero-Shot (ZS) and Few-Shot (FS) settings.
Bar segments:
<span id="S4.F8.8.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">CP-CR (Correct Prediction &amp; Correct Reason)</span>,
<span id="S4.F8.8.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#CCCC00;">CP-ICR (Correct Prediction &amp; Incorrect Reason)</span>,
<span id="S4.F8.8.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B30000;">ICP-ICR (Incorrect Prediction &amp; Incorrect Reason)</span>.
</span></figcaption><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F8.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="286.42" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 339.33 286.42" width="339.33"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,286.42) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(109.22,0) translate(0,33.85) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -109.21 -33.85)"><g class="ltx_nestedsvg" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0) translate(109.22,0) translate(0,43.91)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 5.91 0 M 0 22.34 L 5.91 22.34 M 0 44.68 L 5.91 44.68 M 0 67.03 L 5.91 67.03 M 0 89.37 L 5.91 89.37 M 0 111.71 L 5.91 111.71 M 0 134.05 L 5.91 134.05 M 0 156.4 L 5.91 156.4 M 0 178.74 L 5.91 178.74 M 0 201.08 L 5.91 201.08"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -10.05 L 217.23 -10.05"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -10.05 L 0 211.14"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -2.76 -21.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.49em;--ltx-fo-height:0.55em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:8.19pt;" height="6.24" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.24)" width="5.52"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F8.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 30.69 -21.19)"><